# Working rules

This is a live ecommerce CMS. Preserve current features, add-ons, routes, views, and data flows.

## Safety

- Prefer the smallest change that solves the request. Reuse existing helpers, components, and patterns.
- Do not refactor, rename, or restyle neighboring code as part of an unrelated fix.
- Before finishing, check related pages and shared state: empty lists, validation errors, guest vs logged-in, and admin vs seller when both use the same flow.
- Do not leave debug probes in the tree (`file_put_contents` to `debug-*.log`, `#region agent log`, temporary `try/catch` loggers).

## Database

- Do not create, edit, or generate Laravel migration files.
- Do not run `php artisan migrate`, `migrate:fresh`, `migrate:refresh`, `migrate:rollback`, `db:seed`, or any SQL/schema change against the live or local database.
- Prefer code that works with the existing schema. When a model already has `tableReady()`, keep that guard.
- If a change still needs a table or column, do the application work and put the raw SQL at the end of the reply for the user to run. Do not execute that SQL.

## Laravel

Follow the masters already in this app (tax, batch, discount, product). Do not introduce a new layer.

- Admin controllers: permission middleware in the constructor, validate with an existing `FormRequest` when one exists, then `flash(translate('...'))` and redirect.
- Put create/update writes in the existing service when the feature already has one (`ProductService`, `OrderService`, `CartService`). Do not add a service for a one-off change.
- Listing filters, sort maps, and `tableReady()` belong on the model when that model already owns them (`applyListingFilters`, `resolveSort`).
- Gate optional features with `addon_is_activated('...')`. Do not assume an add-on is on.
- User-facing strings go through `translate()`.

## Admin and storefront UI

- Backend screens live under `resources/views/backend/...`. Copy the nearest sibling `index`, `create`, or `edit` blade instead of inventing a layout.
- Sidebar links use `aiz-side-nav-link` and `areActiveRoutes([...])`. Place a new item in the menu section the user named; do not rebuild the nav.
- Change only the label, tooltip, filter, or column that was requested. Keep existing filter query params and pagination (`withQueryString()`).
- Storefront and admin must stay consistent when they share a model, route name, or helper (`translate`, `static_asset`, `my_asset`).
- After a UI change, check the empty state, a validation error, and both a wide and a narrow viewport when the layout moved.

## Verify, then report

- For UI, layout, routing, or rendered data, exercise the changed flow in the browser before calling it done: click, type, submit, and open every page that shares the state.
- If the browser is unavailable, use the closest substitute (a request against the local server) and say what was not verified.
- Every completed change ends with a numbered list a person can follow: URL, clicks, and the expected result. Include the local server start command when one is needed.

## Git

- Do not commit, push, or open a pull request unless the user asks.
- Never commit `.env`, credentials, `vendor/`, `public/uploads`, or `debug-*.log`.
- When asked to commit, use a short message about why, in the same plain style as recent history (for example `entry side bar menu, product forms fix`).

## EOD Work list
- when EOD worklist is requested repond with a simple plain list of tasks completed today, no jargon.

## Token ethics
- use only the required flex for work
- if a rule added task like testing or browser walk through is estimated to consume high amount of tokens suggest a manual work around
